minkowski-metric-cartesian.conceptual_definition:
The Minkowski metric tensor, denoted by 
er_{ij}
er, is a fundamental object in special relativity that defines the spacetime interval between two events. In Cartesian coordinates and for a flat spacetime (Minkowski spacetime), the metric tensor simplifies significantly. The equation 
er_{ij} = 
er_{ij}
er indicates that in this specific coordinate system, the metric tensor is equivalent to the Kronecker delta, which is a simple matrix with ones on the main diagonal and zeros elsewhere.

minkowski-metric-cartesian.limits_and_boundary:
This specific form of the Minkowski metric, 
er_{ij} = 
er_{ij}
er, is characteristic of flat spacetime. It does not change with position or direction. In the presence of gravity, spacetime becomes curved, and the metric tensor 
er_{ij}
er becomes a non-constant function of spacetime coordinates, deviating from the simple Kronecker delta form. Therefore, this equation represents the metric in the limit of zero gravitational fields and in a coordinate system that aligns with the intrinsic flatness of spacetime.
